{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "cues": [
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-1",
      "transcript": "About two billion years ago, Earth had nuclear reactors. Nobody built them.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-1.wav",
      "start": 0,
      "duration": 5.35,
      "beatIndex": 0,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.1",
      "samples": 128400,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-2",
      "transcript": "In 1972, uranium from Gabon showed an anomaly: less uranium-235 than expected.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-2.wav",
      "start": 5.666666666666667,
      "duration": 8.575,
      "beatIndex": 1,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.2",
      "samples": 205800,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-3",
      "transcript": "The rock also carried fission's isotope fingerprints. Uranium nuclei had split. But how could rock keep that going?",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-3.wav",
      "start": 14.433333333333334,
      "duration": 8.075,
      "beatIndex": 2,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.3",
      "samples": 193800,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-4",
      "transcript": "Ancient uranium had a higher share of uranium-235, an isotope that splits readily. Geology concentrated the uranium into rich deposits.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-4.wav",
      "start": 22.833333333333332,
      "duration": 11.7,
      "beatIndex": 3,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.4",
      "samples": 280800,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-5",
      "transcript": "Each split released neutrons that could split more uranium. Groundwater helped: collisions with hydrogen slowed those neutrons, making further splits more likely.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-5.wav",
      "start": 34.7,
      "duration": 11.1,
      "beatIndex": 4,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.5",
      "samples": 266400,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-6",
      "transcript": "Enough neutrons stayed in the deposit instead of escaping or being absorbed elsewhere. The chain sustained itself.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-6.wav",
      "start": 45.96666666666667,
      "duration": 7.95,
      "beatIndex": 5,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.6",
      "samples": 190800,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-7",
      "transcript": "In one studied zone, isotope evidence suggests repeated stops and starts. Researchers infer that heating drove water away; cooling let it return.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-7.wav",
      "start": 54.1,
      "duration": 10.35,
      "beatIndex": 6,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.7",
      "samples": 248400,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    },
    {
      "id": "oklo-v4-cue-8",
      "transcript": "These were reaction zones in rock. Heat, not electricity. Earth had assembled the fuel, water and geology, long before machinery.",
      "file": "audio/oklo-v4/narration/cue-8.wav",
      "start": 64.76666666666667,
      "duration": 9.35,
      "beatIndex": 7,
      "sourceScriptSegmentId": "oklo.asset.natural-reactors.script.8",
      "samples": 224400,
      "sampleRate": 24000
    }
  ],
